## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about memory access patterns and cache locality in a practical loop, not just algorithmic correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Cache locality
- Blocking/tiling
- L1 data reuse
- Edge-case handling

## Code Example
```javascript
void transpose(int N, int M, const int A[][M], int B[][N]) {
  const int BS = 16;
  for (int r = 0; r < N; r += BS)
    for (int c = 0; c < M; c += BS) {
      int rmax = (r + BS > N) ? N : r + BS;
      int cmax = (c + BS > M) ? M : c + BS;
      for (int i = r; i < rmax; ++i)
        for (int j = c; j < cmax; ++j)
          B[j][i] = A[i][j];
    }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you benchmark the cache miss rate vs a naive row-major transpose?
- How does tile size affect performance on different CPU architectures?

## Why This Is Asked

Persistent memory requires crash-safe state transitions. This task tests ability to design data structures that survive power loss, with proper ordering and visibility guarantees across multiple producers and consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Persistent memory semantics and durable flush boundaries (clwb/psnc, SFENCE)
- Per-slot sequence counters to distinguish empty/full states
- CAS-based head/tail reservation and release semantics
- Epoch-based reclamation for safe memory reuse

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
bool pmq_enqueue(PMQ *q, void *payload) {
  uint64_t h = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[h & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != h) return false;
  memcpy(s->payload, payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, h+1, memory_order_release);
  // flush payload and seq to NVM, ensure visibility
  clwb((void*)s, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  SFENCE();
  return true;
}

// Pseudo-dequeue (simplified)
bool pmq_dequeue(PMQ *q, void *out) {
  uint64_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&q->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  Slot *s = &q->slots[t & (N-1)];
  if (s->seq != t+1) return false;
  memcpy(out, s->payload, PAYLOAD_SIZE);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, t+N+1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&q->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this design for power-failure resume without data loss? 
- How to choose N and payload size for latency/throughput trade-offs in a busy IPC path?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization, reusable barriers, and memory ordering with atomics.

## Key Concepts
- C11 atomics and memory_order semantics
- Sense-reversing barrier pattern
- Reusability and performance on multi-core CPUs

## Code Example
```javascript
// skeleton in C-like pseudocode
typedef struct {
  _Atomic int count;
  int total;
  _Atomic int sense;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n){ b->count = 0; b->total = n; atomic_store(&b->sense, 0); }

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int *local_sense){
  int my = *local_sense;
  int arrived = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel);
  if (arrived == b->total - 1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    *local_sense = 1 - my;
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->sense, 1 - atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_relaxed), memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_acquire) == my) { /* spin */ }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic thread counts?
- How would NUMA-aware placements affect performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory layout, overlap handling, and cache-friendly copy strategies in tight, real-world paths.

## Key Concepts
- Overlap-safe memmove
- Cache-friendly 64-byte blocks
- Alignment assumptions and tail handling
- Fallback to memcpy for tiny or unaligned data
- Microbenchmark plan to measure throughput vs standard memcpy

## Code Example
```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *memmove64(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n){
  unsigned char *d = (unsigned char*)dst;
  const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char*)src;
  const size_t BLOCK = 64;

  if ((size_t)(d) <= (size_t)(s) || d >= s + n){
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      d += BLOCK; s += BLOCK; n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d, s, n);
  } else {
    d += n; s += n;
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      d -= BLOCK; s -= BLOCK;
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d - n, s - n, n);
  }
  return dst;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for arbitrary alignment and larger data sizes?
- Compare performance to builtin memmove on x86-64 with different runtimes.
- What edge cases or architectures could break this approach?

